To enable your Node.js application to communicate with the Redis cluster, you'll need to follow these steps:

1. Install a Redis client library for Node.js: There are several Redis client libraries available for Node.js. The most commonly used one is `redis`. You can install it using npm:
```bash
npm install redis
```
1. Configure the connection to the Redis cluster: You'll need to provide the connection details of your Redis cluster (hostname or IP address and port) to the Node.js application. For this, you can use environment variables or a configuration file.

For example, you can create a `.env` file in your project's root directory with the following content:
```makefile
REDIS_HOST=your-redis-hostname
REDIS_PORT=6379
```
Then, use the `dotenv` package to load the environment variables in your Node.js application:
```bash
npm install dotenv
```
In your Node.js application, load the environment variables:
```javascript
require('dotenv').config();
```
1. Connect to the Redis cluster and use it in your Node.js application: Use the `redis` library to establish a connection to the Redis cluster using the provided hostname and port. Here's an example:
```javascript
const redis = require('redis');
const { promisify } = require('util');

// Load environment variables
require('dotenv').config();

const client = redis.createClient({
  host: process.env.REDIS_HOST,
  port: process.env.REDIS_PORT,
});

// Convert Redis client methods to use promises
const getAsync = promisify(client.get).bind(client);
const setAsync = promisify(client.set).bind(client);

// Example usage
async function example() {
  await setAsync('key', 'value');
  const value = await getAsync('key');
  console.log(`Retrieved value from Redis: ${value}`);
}

example().catch((error) => {
  console.error('Error:', error);
});
```
This example demonstrates how to connect to the Redis cluster using the provided hostname and port, and how to perform basic `get` and `set` operations using the `redis` library.

Replace `your-redis-hostname` with the actual hostname or IP address of the primary node in your Redis cluster, and update the port number if necessary.

With this setup, your Node.js application can communicate with the Redis cluster to perform various operations such as caching, pub/sub, and more.

In the provided CDK example, we've created two Auto Scaling groups, but we haven't set up any service discovery mechanism between them. To enable service discovery, you can use AWS Cloud Map, which allows you to create custom names for your application resources and maintain an up-to-date view of their locations.

Here's how to update the CDK script to include Cloud Map service discovery for the Redis Auto Scaling group:

1. Install the AWS Cloud Map package:
```bash
npm install @aws-cdk/aws-servicediscovery
```
1. Update your CDK script to create a namespace and service for your Redis instances:
```typescript
import * as servicediscovery from '@aws-cdk/aws-servicediscovery';

// ...

// Create a Cloud Map namespace for service discovery
const namespace = new servicediscovery.PrivateDnsNamespace(this, 'Namespace', {
  name: 'example.local',
  vpc,
});

// Create a Cloud Map service for the Redis instances
const redisService = new servicediscovery.Service(this, 'RedisService', {
  namespace,
  name: 'redis',
});
```
1. Enable service discovery for the Redis Auto Scaling group:
```typescript
const redisASG = new autoscaling.AutoScalingGroup(this, 'RedisASG', {
  // ...
  cloudMapOptions: {
    service: redisService,
  },
});
```
Now, your Redis instances will automatically register and deregister with the Cloud Map service when they are created or terminated by the Auto Scaling group. You can use the custom domain name `redis.example.local` to discover the Redis instances from your application.

In your Node.js application, you can use the same `redis.example.local` domain name to connect to the Redis cluster. Update your `.env` file to include the Cloud Map service domain:
```makefile
REDIS_HOST=redis.example.local
REDIS_PORT=6379
```
With this configuration, your Node.js application will be able to discover the Redis instances using their Cloud Map service domain name and establish a connection to perform various operations. Note that you might need to configure your Node.js Redis client library to support connecting to multiple Redis instances if required.

To implement role-based access control in your CDK script, you can use security groups to restrict access to the Redis Auto Scaling Group (ASG). For this example, we'll create a security group for the Redis ASG and allow access only from the EC2 instances in the web application ASG that have a specific role.

1. Create a security group for the Redis ASG:
```typescript
const redisSecurityGroup = new ec2.SecurityGroup(this, 'RedisSecurityGroup', {
  vpc,
  description: 'Allow access to Redis from web application instances',
});
```
1. Update the Redis ASG to use the new security group:
```typescript
const redisASG = new autoscaling.AutoScalingGroup(this, 'RedisASG', {
  // ...
  securityGroup: redisSecurityGroup,
});
```
1. Create a role for the web application instances:
```typescript
import * as iam from '@aws-cdk/aws-iam';

const webAppInstanceRole = new iam.Role(this, 'WebAppInstanceRole', {
  assumedBy: new iam.ServicePrincipal('ec2.amazonaws.com'),
});
```
1. Add necessary permissions to the role. For example, you can attach the AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore managed policy to enable the instances to use AWS Systems Manager:
```typescript
webAppInstanceRole.addManagedPolicy(
  iam.ManagedPolicy.fromAwsManagedPolicyName('AmazonSSMManagedInstanceCore')
);
```
1. Update the web application ASG to use the new role:
```typescript
const webAppASG = new autoscaling.AutoScalingGroup(this, 'WebAppASG', {
  // ...
  role: webAppInstanceRole,
});
```
1. Configure the Redis security group to allow traffic from the web application security group:
```typescript
redisSecurityGroup.addIngressRule(
  ec2.Peer.securityGroup(webAppASG.connections.securityGroups[0]),
  ec2.Port.tcp(6379),
  'Allow access to Redis from web application instances'
);
```
Now, the Redis ASG is accessible only from the instances in the web application ASG with the specified role.

In the Node.js application, you can continue using the same connection details to communicate with the Redis ASG. The role-based access control is enforced at the network level, and the application code does not need to change.

Here's a simple AWS CDK script to create a Lambda function and expose it via API Gateway. This example assumes you have a Node.js Lambda function in a directory called `lambda`.

1. First, create a new directory for your CDK project and initialize the project:
```bash
mkdir my-cdk-project
cd my-cdk-project
cdk init app --language typescript
```
1. Install the necessary CDK packages:
```bash
npm install @aws-cdk/aws-lambda @aws-cdk/aws-apigateway
```
1. Replace the content of `lib/my_cdk_project-stack.ts` with the following TypeScript code:
```typescript
import * as cdk from '@aws-cdk/core';
import * as lambda from '@aws-cdk/aws-lambda';
import * as apigateway from '@aws-cdk/aws-apigateway';

export class MyCdkProjectStack extends cdk.Stack {
  constructor(scope: cdk.Construct, id: string, props?: cdk.StackProps) {
    super(scope, id, props);

    // Create a Lambda function
    const myLambda = new lambda.Function(this, 'MyLambdaFunction', {
      runtime: lambda.Runtime.NODEJS_14_X,
      handler: 'index.handler',
      code: lambda.Code.fromAsset('lambda'),
    });

    // Create an API Gateway REST API
    const api = new apigateway.RestApi(this, 'MyApi', {
      restApiName: 'My Lambda API',
    });

    // Create a default integration for the Lambda function
    const lambdaIntegration = new apigateway.LambdaIntegration(myLambda);

    // Create a default API Gateway resource and method
    api.root.addMethod('GET', lambdaIntegration);
  }
}
```
This code creates a simple infrastructure with a single Lambda function and exposes it via API Gateway. The Lambda function uses the Node.js 14.x runtime, and its code is loaded from the `lambda` directory. The API Gateway is configured to have a single GET method that triggers the Lambda function.

1. Make sure your `lambda/index.js` file contains the handler function:
```javascript
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const response = {
    statusCode: 200,
    body: JSON.stringify('Hello from Lambda!'),
  };
  return response;
};
```
1. Build and deploy the CDK stack:
```bash
npm run build
cdk deploy
```
After the deployment is complete, you'll see the API Gateway URL in the output. You can use this URL to test your API, which triggers the Lambda function.

To replace the Lambda function with an EC2 instance hosting a simple Node.js service, you need to create an EC2 instance, a security group to allow traffic to the instance, and modify the API Gateway integration. This example assumes you have the Node.js service code and an `app.js` entry point file for your service.

1. Install the necessary CDK packages:
```bash
npm install @aws-cdk/aws-ec2
```
1. Replace the content of `lib/my_cdk_project-stack.ts` with the following TypeScript code:
```typescript
import * as cdk from '@aws-cdk/core';
import * as ec2 from '@aws-cdk/aws-ec2';
import * as apigateway from '@aws-cdk/aws-apigateway';

export class MyCdkProjectStack extends cdk.Stack {
  constructor(scope: cdk.Construct, id: string, props?: cdk.StackProps) {
    super(scope, id, props);

    // Create a VPC
    const vpc = new ec2.Vpc(this, 'MyVpc', {
      maxAzs: 1,
    });

    // Create a security group for the EC2 instance
    const ec2SecurityGroup = new ec2.SecurityGroup(this, 'Ec2SecurityGroup', {
      vpc,
      description: 'Allow traffic to the EC2 instance',
    });

    // Allow traffic to the Node.js service on port 3000
    ec2SecurityGroup.addIngressRule(ec2.Peer.anyIpv4(), ec2.Port.tcp(3000), 'Allow traffic on port 3000');

    // Create an Amazon Linux 2 instance with Node.js installed
    const ec2Instance = new ec2.Instance(this, 'MyEc2Instance', {
      instanceType: ec2.InstanceType.of(ec2.InstanceClass.T3, ec2.InstanceSize.NANO),
      machineImage: new ec2.AmazonLinuxImage({
        generation: ec2.AmazonLinuxGeneration.AMAZON_LINUX_2,
      }),
      vpc,
      securityGroup: ec2SecurityGroup,
      keyName: 'my-key-pair', // Replace this with the name of your EC2 key pair
    });

    // Userdata script to install Node.js, copy the service code, and start the service
    ec2Instance.userData.addCommands(
      'curl --silent --location https://rpm.nodesource.com/setup_14.x | sudo bash -',
      'sudo yum -y install nodejs',
      'mkdir /home/ec2-user/nodejs-service',
      'cp /tmp/asset-*/app.js /home/ec2-user/nodejs-service/', // Replace with the appropriate asset path for your service
      'cd /home/ec2-user/nodejs-service',
      'nohup node app.js > output.log 2>&1 &'
    );

    // Create an API Gateway REST API
    const api = new apigateway.RestApi(this, 'MyApi', {
      restApiName: 'My EC2 API',
    });

    // Create an HTTP integration for the EC2 instance
    const ec2InstanceIntegration = new apigateway.HttpIntegration(`http://${ec2Instance.instancePublicIp}:3000`);

    // Create a default API Gateway resource and method
    api.root.addMethod('GET', ec2InstanceIntegration);
  }
}
```
